To find the electrostatic potential Φ(z) to the lowest non-zero power of z, we can use the Taylor expansion formula. Since z\ll R, we can assume that the distance from the center of the ring to the point z is approximately R, and the distance from the center of the ring to the point z is approximately R. We can apply the Taylor expansion formula to the expression for Φ(z):

\Phi(z)=\frac{q}{4\pi\varepsilon_{0}}\frac{1}{\sqrt{R^{2}+z^{2}}}
\approx\frac{q}{4\pi\varepsilon_{0}}\frac{1}{\sqrt{R^{2}+R^{2}}}
=\frac{q}{4\pi\varepsilon_{0}}\frac{1}{R}
\approx\frac{q}{4\pi\varepsilon_{0}R}

However, we still need to account for the z term in the denominator. We can do this by using the Taylor expansion formula again:

\sqrt{R^{2}+R^{2}}\approx R+\frac{1}{2}R^{2}
\frac{1}{\sqrt{R^{2}+R^{2}}}\approx\frac{1}{R+\frac{1}{2}R^{2}}
\approx1-\frac{1}{2}R

Substituting this expression back into the original equation for Φ(z), we get:

\Phi(z)=\frac{q}{4\pi\varepsilon_{0}}\frac{1}{R+\frac{1}{2}R^{2}}
\approx\frac{q}{4\pi\varepsilon_{0}}\left(1-\frac{1}{2}R\right)
\approx\frac{q}{4\pi\varepsilon_{0}}\left(1-\frac{z^{2}}{2R^{2}}\right)

